<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Swat State</h1><p class="st-meta">1915 CE – 1969 CE · capital Saidu Sharif</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A 20th-century Pashtun kingdom in the Swat valley, built by the Wali Miangul Abdul Wadud and abolished in 1969.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1915 the Yusufzai tribes of Swat chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-abdul-jabbar-shah">Sayyid Abdul Jabbar Shah</a> as their ruler. He was replaced in 1917 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miangul-abdul-wadud">Miangul Abdul Wadud</a>, grandson of the revered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhund-of-swat">Akhund of Swat</a>, who united the valley by force and diplomacy. The British recognised him as Wali in 1926.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miangul-abdul-wadud">Abdul Wadud</a>, known as Badshah Sahib, built a centralised state with a standing army, telephone lines, roads and forts, ruling according to Islamic law and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtun</a> custom. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miangul-jahanzeb">Miangul Jahanzeb</a>, Wali from 1949, expanded schools, hospitals and colleges at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saidu-sharif">Saidu Sharif</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Swat acceded to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947 but kept internal autonomy until 1969, when it was abolished and merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">North-West Frontier Province</a>, now Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/swat-state.jpg" alt="Swat State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Washiucho · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Swat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1915 CE – 1969 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saidu Sharif</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abdul Jabbar Shah, Miangul Abdul Wadud, Miangul Jahanzeb</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pashto</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yusufzai State of Swat, Swat</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=swat-state" aria-label="Swat State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6516 920 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Swat State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6836 1176L7364 1602M6836 1176L6778 1519"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1602h0M6778 1519h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6836 1176h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6836 1176h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saidu Sharif. <a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=swat-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

